items.strategy:
3.1 The operators are well defined. Since $\ell(sx)=\ell(x)\pm1$ for all $x\in W$ and $s\in S$ ([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]], part 1), the two clauses are exhaustive and mutually exclusive, so each $e_w$ has exactly one image; $R$-linearity is then imposed. Both clauses have the form $e_x\mapsto e_{\sigma x}+c\,e_x$, and no selection is involved.

3.2 Commutation: the six configurations (Lusztig, Proposition 3.3; locator in the sources). Fix $s,t\in S$ and $w\in W$. Since each of $\ell(sw),\ell(wt)$ differs from $\ell(w)$ by $\pm1$, and $\ell(swt)$ differs from both $\ell(sw)$ and $\ell(wt)$ by $\pm1$, expanding $P_sQ_t(e_w)$ and $Q_tP_s(e_w)$ from 3.1 leaves exactly six length configurations:
(i) $\ell(sw)=\ell(wt)=\ell(w)+1$, $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)+2$: both sides equal $e_{swt}$;
(ii) $\ell(sw)=\ell(wt)=\ell(w)-1$, $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)-2$: both sides equal $e_{swt}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})e_{wt}+(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_{sw}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_w$;
(iii) $\ell(sw)=\ell(w)-1$, $\ell(wt)=\ell(w)+1$, $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)$: both sides equal $e_{swt}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})e_{wt}$;
(iv) $\ell(sw)=\ell(w)+1$, $\ell(wt)=\ell(w)-1$, $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)$: both sides equal $e_{swt}+(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_{sw}$;
(v) $\ell(sw)=\ell(wt)=\ell(w)-1$, $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)$: the two sides are $e_{swt}+(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_{sw}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_w$ and $e_{swt}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})e_{wt}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_w$;
(vi) $\ell(sw)=\ell(wt)=\ell(w)+1$, $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)$: the two sides are $e_{swt}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})e_{wt}$ and $e_{swt}+(v_t-v_t^{-1})e_{sw}$.
In (v) and (vi) the hypotheses $\ell(swt)=\ell(w)$ and $\ell(sw)=\ell(wt)$ of Proposition 1.10 hold, so $sw=wt$ by [[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (exchange, parts 2-3); then $s$ and $t$ are conjugate in $W$, hence $s\sim t$ and $v_s=v_t$ by [[def-hh-universal-coxeter-hecke-parameters-and-presentation]]. The two sides therefore agree in every configuration, i.e. $P_sQ_t(e_w)=Q_tP_s(e_w)$; since this holds for every basis vector $e_w$, $P_sQ_t=Q_tP_s$. (The computation shows more than is needed: in (v) and (vi) the two sides agree as soon as $v_s-v_s^{-1}=v_t-v_t^{-1}$; this refinement is recorded on the examples page, [[ex-hh-unequal-parameter-dihedral-consistency]].)

3.3 Quadratics. If $\ell(sw)=\ell(w)+1$ then $P_s^2(e_w)=P_s(e_{sw})=e_w+(v_s-v_s^{-1})e_{sw}$; if $\ell(sw)=\ell(w)-1$ then $P_s(e_w)=e_{sw}+(v_s-v_s^{-1})e_w$ and $\ell(s\cdot sw)=\ell(w)=\ell(sw)+1$, so $P_s(e_{sw})=e_w$ and $P_s^2(e_w)=e_w+(v_s-v_s^{-1})P_s(e_w)$. Hence $P_s^2=(v_s-v_s^{-1})P_s+\operatorname{id}_E$ on the basis, and $P_s(P_s-(v_s-v_s^{-1})\operatorname{id}_E)=\operatorname{id}_E=(P_s-(v_s-v_s^{-1})\operatorname{id}_E)P_s$. The computation with $Q_s$ is the mirror image.

3.4 Braid relations. Let $m:=m(s,t)<\infty$ and let $a:=P_sP_tP_s\cdots$, $b:=P_tP_sP_t\cdots$, with $m$ factors each. By the ambient reducedness of alternating words ([[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], part 7) the alternating words of length $k\le m$ are reduced in the ambient sense, so applying the factors of $a$ successively to $e_1$ lands on basis vectors and gives $a(e_1)=e_{w_1}$, where $w_1$ is the alternating product with $m$ factors beginning with $s$; likewise $b(e_1)=e_{w_2}$ for the other alternating product. In $W$ one has $w_1=w_2$: for $m=2k+1$ the two alternating words are $(st)^ks$ and $(ts)^kt$, and the computation of [[def-hh-universal-coxeter-hecke-parameters-and-presentation]], strategy 1.3 (with $s^2=t^2=1$ and $(st)^m=1$), gives $(st)^ks=(ts)^kt$; for $m=2k$ they are $(st)^k$ and $(ts)^k=(st)^{-k}=(st)^{m-k}=(st)^k$, using $(st)^m=1$. Hence $a(e_1)=b(e_1)$. Now let $w=u_1\cdots u_k$ be reduced. Each prefix of a reduced word is reduced, so $Q_{u_k}\cdots Q_{u_1}(e_1)=e_w$. Since every $P_s$ commutes with every $Q_t$ by 3.2, $a(e_w)=aQ_{u_k}\cdots Q_{u_1}(e_1)=Q_{u_k}\cdots Q_{u_1}a(e_1)=Q_{u_k}\cdots Q_{u_1}b(e_1)=b(e_w)$. As $(e_w)$ is a basis, $a=b$. The $Q$-case is symmetric.

3.5 Reduced products. Every contiguous subword of a reduced expression is reduced, since any shortening would shorten the entire expression. For $w=s_1\cdots s_k$, put $z_i=s_i\cdots s_k$ and $z_{k+1}=1$. Then $\ell(z_i)=k-i+1$ and $s_i z_{i+1}=z_i$, so applying $P_{s_k},\dots,P_{s_1}$ successively gives $P_{s_1}\cdots P_{s_k}(e_1)=e_w$. The corresponding prefix computation gives $Q_{s_k}\cdots Q_{s_1}(e_1)=e_w$. Matsumoto and the braid relations then give reduced-word independence.

3.6 Choice. None is used: the operators are defined by explicit length conditions, and all products are along reduced expressions that already exist by the definition of $\ell$.
